𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟭𝟬 𝗼𝗳 𝟯𝟭: 𝗥𝗘𝗦𝗧 𝗔𝗣𝗜𝘀 🔗

REST is the most widely used API style on the internet — stateless, resource-based, and built entirely on standard HTTP verbs.

Every resource gets a URL. Every action maps to a verb:

🟢 GET /users/123 → fetch a user
🔵 POST /users → create a user
🟠 PUT /users/123 → update a user
🔴 DELETE /users/123 → delete a user

That's the whole idea. You're not calling custom functions — you're performing standard operations on nouns (resources) using a fixed set of verbs everyone already understands.

𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗶𝘁 𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸𝘀 𝘀𝗼 𝘄𝗲𝗹𝗹

✅ 𝗣𝗿𝗲𝗱𝗶𝗰𝘁𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 — GET /orders/456 always returns the same structure. Any client, in any language, knows exactly what to expect.

✅ 𝗖𝗮𝗰𝗵𝗲𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 — predictable responses tied to a URL mean browsers, CDNs, and proxies can cache them with zero extra logic.

✅ 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝗲𝘀𝘀 — the server never remembers the client between requests, which makes REST APIs easy to scale horizontally.

✅ 𝗨𝗻𝗶𝘃𝗲𝗿𝘀𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆 𝘂𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗿𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗼𝗱 — HTTP verbs and status codes are a shared vocabulary. No custom protocol to learn.

⚠️ 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗱𝗲-𝗼𝗳𝗳

Because the response shape is fixed, clients often get more data than they need (over-fetching) or have to make several calls to stitch together related data (multiple round-trips).

That single limitation is exactly why GraphQL and gRPC exist — both trade REST's simplicity for more flexible or more efficient data fetching. That's where Day 11 picks up. 👇

